{"step_index":113,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:33:34Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-12T01:33:34+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-12T01:33:34+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/tools/prover_eval/w16.py`\nTotal Lines: 354\nTotal Bytes: 15815\nShowing lines 1 to 100\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: #!/usr/bin/env python\n2: \"\"\"tools/prover_eval/w16.py — whole-proof Pass@16, one model, no feedback.\n3: \n4: W16 is the condition that compares against the conventional whole-proof\n5: literature: 16 independent attempts, no compiler feedback, no retrieval, no\n6: cross-attempt communication, and a challenge the model may not edit.\n7: \n8: Each benchmark item supplies *two* declarations over one shared `Candidate`,\n9: so a model may attempt either direction and the item counts as resolved when\n10: either is kernel-verified. Which direction verified is recorded, not collapsed:\n11: against a labelled gold item, a verified proof of the wrong direction is not a\n12: success but evidence that the formalization is unsound, and that is the single\n13: most valuable signal this run can produce.\n14: \n15: Two guards matter more than the score:\n16: \n17:   * every accepted proof is re-checked with the axiom probe, so a `sorryAx`\n18:     reached through a helper lemma cannot pass as a proof;\n19:   * identical candidate proofs are verified once, because 16 samples of a\n20:     deterministic-ish model repeat heavily and Lean is the expensive part.\n21: \n22: Usage:\n23:     python tools/prover_eval/w16.py --model oprover-32b --partition gold\n24: \"\"\"\n25: from __future__ import annotations\n26: \n27: import argparse\n28: import concurrent.futures as cf\n29: import json\n30: import os\n31: import re\n32: import sys\n33: import threading\n34: import time\n35: \n36: sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(\n37:     os.path.abspath(__file__)))))\n38: \n39: from config import CONFIG  # noqa: E402\n40: \n41: PROMPT = \"\"\"You are completing a Lean 4 theorem in a fixed trusted project.\n42: \n43: Return exactly one <answer> block containing only a proof term beginning\n44: with \"by\".\n45: \n46: Do not:\n47: - alter the theorem statement,\n48: - redefine any invariant,\n49: - use sorry or admit,\n50: - declare axioms,\n51: - modify imports,\n52: - use unsafe declarations.\n53: \n54: Trusted Lean source:\n55: \n56: ```lean\n57: {source}\n58: ```\n59: \n60: Complete the declaration `{target}`.\n61: \n62: <answer>\n63: by\n64:   ...\n65: </answer>\n66: \"\"\"\n67: \n68: _ANSWER = re.compile(r\"<answer>(.*?)</answer>\", re.S)\n69: # `lean4` must be tried before `lean`, and the tag must be consumed exactly.\n70: # The original `(?:lean)?\\s*` matched \"lean\" inside \"```lean4\" and then stopped,\n71: # because \"4\" is not whitespace — so the captured body began \"4\\n...\" and failed\n72: # every downstream check. OProver-8B writes ```lean4 in 60% of replies and\n73: # DeepSeek-7B in 57%, while Goedel-32B writes plain ```lean in 96%, so the bug\n74: # penalised models by which tag they happened to use. Re-scoring every saved\n75: # reply through the fix moved candidate counts a lot (DeepSeek 72 -> 102,\n76: # OProver-8B 11 -> 25) but changed no model's resolution count: Goedel-32B still\n77: # 3, everyone else still 0. The ranking was not an artefact of this regex.\n78: _FENCE = re.compile(r\"```(?:lean4|lean)?[^\\S\\n]*\\n?(.*?)```\", re.S)\n79: # Reasoning models (OProver and Goedel are Qwen3-style) spend tokens inside\n80: # <think> before answering. Left in place it swamps the parse — and if the token\n81: # budget expires mid-thought there is no answer at all, which is why the budget\n82: # has to be generous for these models rather than the 4096 a one-shot prover\n83: # would need.\n84: _THINK = re.compile(r\"<think>.*?</think>\", re.S)\n85: _OPEN_THINK = re.compile(r\"<think>.*\", re.S)\n86: _BY_TAIL = re.compile(r\"^\\s*(by\\b.*)$\", re.S)\n87: # Anything that would make a \"proof\" vacuous. The axiom probe catches these\n88: # even when they are reached indirectly, but rejecting them at the source is\n89: # cheaper and gives a clearer status than a failed audit.\n90: _BANNED = re.compile(r\"\\b(sorry|admit|axiom|unsafe|native_decide)\\b\")\n91: \n92: \n93: def extract_proof(text: str):\n94:     \"\"\"The proof term from a model reply, or None.\n95: \n96:     Tried in order of how much the reply respected the contract: an explicit\n97:     <answer> block, then a fenced Lean block, then a bare `by ...` tail. The\n98:     fallbacks matter — scoring a model as zero because it wrote a correct proof\n99:     in a code fence rather than the requested tag would measure this parser\n100:     rather than the prover.\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
